  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Segment 1D signal into overlapping temporal frames and uniformly quantize amplitudes into discrete integer levels.\n\nShort-time windowing segments non-stationary acoustic signals into quasi-stationary temporal frames for Fourier and autoregressive modeling.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nF_k = [x_{k \\cdot H}, \\dots, x_{k \\cdot H + W - 1}], \\quad Q = \\text{clamp}\\left(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x - x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max} - x_{\\min}} \\cdot L \\right\\rfloor, 0, L - 1\\right)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Extract overlapping temporal windows of length frame_size with step hop_size\n2. Normalize framed signals into range [0, 1]\n3. Bin normalized values into num_levels discrete bins [0, num_levels - 1]\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'num_frames', 'framed', and 'quantized'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Quantize and Frame\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nRunning large AI models in production requires massive computing power. During text generation, memory bandwidth (fetching weights and past key-value caches from VRAM) is often the primary bottleneck rather than raw arithmetic compute. **Quantize and Frame** optimizes hardware efficiency, memory consumption, or request serving throughput.\n\nShort-time windowing segments non-stationary acoustic signals into quasi-stationary temporal frames for Fourier and autoregressive modeling. In this problem, the goal is to: segment 1d signal into overlapping temporal frames and uniformly quantize amplitudes into discrete integer levels.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nF_k = [x_{k \\cdot H}, \\dots, x_{k \\cdot H + W - 1}], \\quad Q = \\text{clamp}\\left(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{x - x_{\\min}}{x_{\\max} - x_{\\min}} \\cdot L \\right\\rfloor, 0, L - 1\\right)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Extract overlapping temporal windows of length frame_size with step hop_size\n2. **Phase 2**: Normalize framed signals into range [0, 1]\n3. **Phase 3**: Bin normalized values into num_levels discrete bins [0, num_levels - 1]\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Memory Bandwidth Bottleneck**: Quantizing weights to INT8 or INT4 cuts memory traffic in half or quarters, dramatically increasing token generation speeds.\n* **Serving Systems**: Dynamic batching and PagedAttention maximize GPU utilization by grouping requests arriving at different times.\n* **Latency vs. Throughput**: In production serving, systems engineers balance Time-To-First-Token (TTFT) against overall tokens-per-second throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: signal = [0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2, -0.4, 0.8, -0.2, 0.0], frame_size = 4, hop_size = 2, num_levels = 4\n\nOutput: {\"num_frames\": 3, \"framed\": [[0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.2], [0.9, 0.2, -0.4, 0.8], [-0.4, 0.8, -0.2, 0.0]], \"quantized\": [[1, 2, 3, 1], [3, 1, 0, 3], [0, 3, 0, 1]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated numpy_quantize_frame under standard test conditions."
